  <activityDataset>
    <activityDescription>
      <activity id="509731b1-79cc-5bf9-89b3-71405cc74139" activityNameId="37bdb7e4-50fb-5d65-8980-ffad697f0786" activityNameContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83" type="1" specialActivityType="0" energyValues="0">
        <activityName xml:lang="en">Diode, glass-, through-hole mounting, at plant</activityName>
        <generalComment>
          <text xml:lang="en" index="1">Full activity name: Diode, glass-, through-hole mounting, at plant
The data represent a typical glass diode for hole-through mounting, used in the information and communication technology. Material data are taken from datasheets of producers of such diodes and from the literature. Infrastructure and production efforts are based on own assumptions. The dataset represents 1 kg of diodes with a weight of 0.596 gram/unit. For all diode types, it is assumed the the material composition remains the same over the years (2023 updates).; UUID: 6f995c12-0321-3786-b8e4-d3ab43c3f31d
This dataset covers raw material input and production efforts for the production of currently used glass diodes for hole-through mounting technology.
Location: Data represent a global valuable composition of through-hole type glass diodes.
Technology: average diode production technology, comprising the diode wafer production (cleaing, masking, etching, doping, oxidising, metal deposition) and the final assembly of the diode (wafer sawing, die bonding, moulding, trimming and forming)
Time period: Unspecified
Production volume: 
Sampling: literature
Extrapolations: see Geography &amp; Technology
Uncertainty adjustments: None
BAFU mapped export: unsupported biosphere exchanges excluded; see audit/. Local UUIDs and generic activity classification are export conventions. Source: process_6f995c12-0321-3786-b8e4-d3ab43c3f31d.xml</text>
        </generalComment>
      </activity>
      <geography geographyId="f4808887-5a57-5892-975e-ba0565a93e23" geographyContextId="f44c43c1-5cc5-54a6-ade6-24435bd12c83">
        <shortname xml:lang="en">GLO</shortname>
        <comment>
